The robot-body model itself carries one update from this work. The adductor magnus attachment point P2 was restored to its full-precision location $(-0.1357, -0.0929, 0.0591)$~m, displacing a rounded copy that was disqualified for producing a zero-length via point that nullified the flexion torque; the regenerated mirrored model passed its validation with zero non-mirrored pairs. At the restored location, the muscle delivers \qty{34.28}{
ewton\cdot\meter} of flexion torque, $1.12	imes$ the human target of \qty{30.61}{
ewton\cdot\meter}, and \qty{27.62}{
ewton\cdot\meter} of adduction torque, $0.82	imes$ the human target of \qty{33.60}{
ewton\cdot\meter}. These redesigned routes remain optimizer-stage results: the robot-body OpenSim model contains no pulley entities, and no via points or markers were added to it for these routes (its eight pre-existing moving-path-point vias, the rectus-femoris and vastii patella-tracking points, one per muscle per leg, belong to the original muscle paths). The MuJoCo conversion used for the simulation work predates the pulley batch and the adductor-magnus restoration, so it carries one stale path point, the pre-restoration rounded adductor-magnus P2. Physical torque tests of the redesigned routes have begun (Section~ef{sec:disc_pulley}).

The route redesign of Section~ef{sec:ongoing} placed both \qty{20}{\mm} actuators on direct attachment routes. The Mono/Bi-pulley algorithms extend that search space by allowing the artificial tendon to be reeved over pulleys before insertion, and the flexor pulley run quantifies what the added routing freedom is worth on the knee-flexor route. The pulley variant converged to a collision-feasible layout with zero bound violations (pattern-search exit flag 1) at an objective of 0.08465 under its own objective function, which differs from the direct route's objective of 74.47, so the two values are not commensurable. The best configuration on file reeves the two actuators over a single tackle-line part at a gain fixed at 1:1, so the pulley acts as a redirect rather than a force multiplier; even so, the redirect alone deepened the peak predicted flexion torque from \qty{81.23}{
ewton\cdot\meter} to \qty{85.74}{
ewton\cdot\meter}, a gain of \qty{4.51}{
ewton\cdot\meter} (\qty{5.6}{\percent}), while holding the off-axis torque to at most \qty{0.803}{
ewton\cdot\meter}, under \qty{1}{\percent} of the peak on-axis value; the predicted peaks of the direct and pulley routes exceed the human flexor torque scale of \qty{23.16}{
ewton\cdot\meter} by factors of 3.51 and 3.70. The Bi-pulley batch establishes where that freedom runs out on anatomical routes. Across the five OpenSim muscle sites attempted (soleus, tibialis anterior, rectus femoris, medial gastrocnemius, and biceps femoris short head), every run closed with a nonpositive torque margin (objectives of 0.929 for tibialis anterior, 0.997 for soleus, and 3.42 for rectus femoris with a residual worst-constraint value of 0.242), and the medial-gastrocnemius and biceps-femoris-short-head runs terminated at the infeasibility sentinel (objective 101, worst-constraint value 10, exit flag $-2$). For the four runs that report a finite route-to-route clearance, the binding constraint was geometry rather than torque: minimum pair clearances spanned \qty{4.26}{\mm} to \qty{19.14}{\mm} against the \qty{25}{\mm} separation required between adjacent routes (two \qty{10}{\mm} actuator radii plus a \qty{5}{\mm} bundle margin); the medial-gastrocnemius run records no finite clearance, so its failure rests on the infeasibility sentinel alone. What the two algorithms buy is therefore reach and screening: the mono-pulley converts routing freedom into \qty{4.51}{
ewton\cdot\meter} of additional predicted flexion torque without mechanical advantage and with off-axis torque below \qty{1}{\percent} of the peak, and the bi-pulley sweep screens candidate anatomical sites, showing that its reeved routes do not yet fit these corridors and redirecting the design effort toward clearance-relaxed attachment sites. These pulley solutions are optimizer-stage results; they have not yet been propagated into the OpenSim or MuJoCo model trees used for the simulation work of Sections~ef{sec:preliminary_sim_methods}--ef{sec:preliminary_sim_results}, and the hardware torque tests of the updated compliant system have begun with the extensor route.

Physical testing of the redesigned extensor route has begun on the test stand with the $\diameter$\qty{20}{\mm} actuator. In the first test, on the earlier direct route, three isometric points were held near full extension: measured knee torque of \qty{1.163}{
ewton\meter} at a knee angle of \ang{10}, \qty{7.849}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{11}, and \qty{23.437}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{10}, against hung loads of \qtylist{3.7;25.2;76}{
ewton} at gauge pressures of \qtylist{388.5;388;592}{\kPa}, respectively. The first and third points sit at essentially the same knee angle and span a twentyfold range of held torque, so they begin to map the route's response near extension as pressure and load vary. On the rebuilt cam route, a nine-point isometric sweep then held loads of \qtylist{100;116;126;140;168;173.4;180.2;181;181.5}{
ewton} at knee angles from \ang{-119} to \ang{5} and gauge pressures from \qty{256}{\kPa} to \qty{488}{\kPa}, with the load-cell angle of every hold recorded in the tibia frame and each measured wrench referred to the moving knee center through the adjoint transform already used for the identification data, at the measured reaction-point distance of \qty{259.9}{\mm}. The adjoint-reduced torques are entered for the six most recent holds and span \qty{28.8}{
ewton\meter} to \qty{50.6}{
ewton\meter}, with the reduction of the three earliest holds in progress. The pressures read against the design on their own: the deep-flexion hold at \ang{-119} was carried at \qty{256}{\kPa}, just below the low end of the cam design's predicted \qtyrange{269}{507}{\kPa} requirement band, and the extension holds at \ang{-1} and \ang{5} at \qty{488}{\kPa} and \qty{480}{\kPa} against the \qty{507}{\kPa} worst case, and the route carried every hold of the sweep without nearing the \qty{620}{\kPa} ceiling. The measured-versus-predicted envelope comparison for the sweep, and the corresponding flexor measurements, are pending.
